    text: "Keratoconus / Queratocono\nDefinition: Progressive non-inflammatory corneal ectasia characterised by corneal thinning, protrusion and irregular astigmatism.\nClinical features: Progressive myopia/astigmatism, reduced best-corrected vision, scissoring reflex, corneal thinning, Fleischer ring, Vogt striae, apical scarring in advanced cases.\nWork-up: Refraction, slit lamp, keratometry, corneal topography/tomography, pachymetry, posterior elevation, epithelial mapping when available.\nTreatment overview: Spectacles or contact lenses for visual rehabilitation; corneal cross-linking for documented progression; intracorneal ring segments, phakic IOL or keratoplasty in selected cases.\nRed flags: Rapid progression, acute hydrops, apical scarring, intolerance to contact lenses or advanced thinning.\nSources: AAO PPP and global consensus support tomography-based diagnosis and cross-linking for progressive disease.",
